===Noun===
{{en-noun}}

# {{senseid|en|relation}} ''Chiefly with a [[qualifying#Adjective|qualifying]] [[word#Noun|word]]'': any [[relation]] (especially a [[distant]] one) who is not a [[direct#Adjective|direct]] [[ancestor]] or [[descendant]] but [[part#Noun|part]] of a [[person#Noun|person]]'s [[extended family]]; a [[kinsman]] or [[kinswoman]].
#* {{RQ:Tyndale NT|format=full|Romans|16|7|folio=ccxvj|verso=1|passage=Salute [[w: Andronicus of Pannonia|Andronicus]]&#11805; and [[w:Junia (New Testament person)|Junia]] my '''coſyns'''&#11805; which were preſoners with me alſo&#11805; which are wele taken amonge the apoſtles&#11805; and were in [[w:Jesus|Chriſt]] before me.}}
#* {{RQ:Shakespeare Much Ado About Nothing Q|act=I|scene=ii|sig=B2|page=14|passage=Hovv novv brother, vvhere is my '''coſen''' your ſonne, hath he prouided this muſique?}}
#* {{RQ:Richardson Clarissa|volume=I|letter=VI|chapter=Miss Clarissa Harlowe, to Miss Howe|page=34|passage='''Couſin''' Harlovve, ſaid my aunt Hervey, allovv me to ſay, That my '''couſin''' Clary's prudence may be confided in.}}
#* {{RQ:M. R. Mitford Our Village|volume=II|chapter=The Old House at Aberleigh|page=244|passage=The house and park and a small estate around it were entailed on a distant '''cousin''', and could not be alienated; {{...}}}}
## {{lb|en|specifically}} ''Preceded by an [[ordinal number]], as'' '''[[first#Adjective|first]]''''','' '''[[second#Adjective|second]]''''','' '''[[third#Adjective|third]]''''', etc.'': a person [[descend]]ed from a [[common ancestor]] by the [[same#Adjective|same]] [[number#Noun|number]] of [[generation]]s as another person.
##* {{RQ:Taylor Ductor Dubitantium|volume=I|chapter=Rule 3. The Judicial Law of {{w|Moses}} is Annul'd, or Abrogated, and Retains No Obliging Power either in Whole or in Part over any Christian Prince, Commonwealth, or Person.|para=89|page=318|passage={{...}} I never knevv the marriage of ſecond '''Coſens''' forbidden, but by them vvho at the ſame time forbad the marriage of the firſt: {{...}} And vve find that ''[[w:Isaac|Iſaac]]'' married his ſecond '''Coſen''', and that vvas more for it then ever could be ſaid againſt it.}}
## {{lb|en|specifically}} ''When used without a qualifying word'': the [[child#Noun|child]] of a person's [[parent#Noun|parent]]'s [[brother#Noun|brother]] (that is, an [[uncle#Noun|uncle]]) or [[sister#Noun|sister]] (an [[aunt]]); a [[cousin-german]], a [[first cousin]].
##: {{ux|en|Although we were '''cousins''', we grew up like sisters.}}
##* {{RQ:Shakespeare Richard 2 Q1|act=I|scene=iv|sig=C2|sigend=C2|versoend=1|page=25|passage='''Cooſen''' Aumarle. / Hovv far brought you high Hereford on his vvay? / {{...}} / VVhat ſaid our '''couſin''' vvhen you parted vvith him?}}
##* {{RQ:Stanley History of Philosophy|volume=III|part=4|chapter=III|chaptername=An Explication of the Pythagorick Symbolls. By [[w:Iamblichus|Jamblichus]]|page=120|passage=[O]thers vvho are allied to us at a great diſtance, as the Children of Uncles, or of '''Coſens''', or their Children or ſuch like, reſemble thoſe parts vvhich may be cut off vvithout pain, as Hair, Nailes, and the like.}}
