#* {{RQ:Shakespeare Richard 3 Q1|act=III|scene=iv|sig=G|verso=1|page=58|passage=My noble L[ords] and '''Coſens''' all, good morrovv, / I haue beene long a ſleeper, but I hope / My abſence doth neglect no great deſignes, / VVhich by my preſence might haue been concluded.|footer=Spoken by {{w|Edward IV of England}}’s brother Richard, Duke of Gloucester, later {{w|Richard III of England}}.}}
#* {{RQ:Shakespeare All's Well|act=III|scene=i|page=241|column=1|passage=Therefore vve meruaile much our '''Coſin''' France / VVould in ſo iuſt a buſineſſe, ſhut his boſome / Againſt our borrovving prayers.|footer=Referring to the French monarch.}}
#* {{RQ:Blackstone Commentaries|book=I|chapter=Of the Civil State|page=386|passage=In all vvrits, and commiſſions, and other formal inſtruments, the king, vvhen he mentions any peer of the degree of an earl, alvvays ſtiles him "truſty and vvell beloved '''''couſin''''':" an appellation as antient as the reign of [[w:Henry IV of England|Henry IV]]; vvho being either by his vvife, his mother, or his ſiſters, actually related or allied to every earl in the kingdom, artfully and conſtantly acknovvledged that connexion in all his letters and other public acts; from vvhence the uſage has deſcended to his ſucceſſors, though the reaſon has long ago failed.}}
# {{lb|en|figurative|also|attributive}} Something [[kindred#Adjective|kindred]] or related to something else; a [[relative#Noun|relative]].
#* {{RQ:Topsell Foure-footed Beastes|chapter=Of the Horsse|page=385|passage=The euill habit of the body, is next '''coſin''' to the dropſie, vvhereof though our Ferrers haue had no experience, {{...}}}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|author=Tim Homfray|title=What do they mean&nbsp;…|journal=[[w:TES (magazine)|Times Educational Supplement]]|archiveurl=https://web.archive.org/web/20231111165926/https://www.tes.com/magazine/archive/what-do-they-mean-20|location=London|publisher=TSL Education|date=21 November 2003|issn=0040-7887|oclc=828716645|passage=Partnering, along with its less irritating '''cousin''' "partnership", crops up all over the place, being equally useful to the lazy jargoneer and the lazy policy-maker. It has been said that there is no noun which cannot be verbed; in the same way, there is now nothing, concrete or abstract, which cannot be partnered.}}
#* {{RQ:Time|author=Tessa Berenson|title=NASA Discovers New Earth-like Planet|archiveurl=https://web.archive.org/web/20230320140049/https://time.com/3969886/nasa-discovers-earth-planet/|date=23 July 2015|passage=NASA has discovered an Earth-like planet orbiting around a star, which a NASA researcher called a "bigger, older '''cousin''' to Earth."}}
